      "claim": "Israel’s argument that the Fourth Geneva Convention does not apply because Jordan was not recognised as sovereign over the West Bank is untenable under the Convention’s own scope and the established practice of international humanitarian law.",
      "reasoning": "The Convention’s applicability is triggered by the fact of occupation by a High Contracting Party, not by the existence of a prior recognized sovereign. While the text of Article 2(2) is not quoted here, the principle is reflected in the language of Additional Protocol I (art. 4) and in the Rome Statute’s treatment of the situation as an armed conflict subject to the Convention’s protections. Consequently, the lack of recognised Jordanian sovereignty does not exempt Israel from the Convention’s obligations, and the strongest counter‑argument rests on the same textual and practice‑based foundations cited above.",
